# IMASViz

**IMASViz** is a visualization tool developed within
**Integrated Modelling Analysis Suite** (**IMAS**) for the purposes of
visualizing static and dynamic IMAS data, stored within IMAS
**Interface Data Structures** (**IDSs**).

Copyright holders : Commissariat � l�Energie Atomique et aux Energies Alternatives (CEA), France;
and Laboratory for Engineering Design - LECAD, University of Ljubljana, Slovenia
CEA and LECAD authorize the use of the METIS software under the CeCILL-C open source license https://cecill.info/licences/Licence_CeCILL-C_V1-en.html
The terms and conditions of the CeCILL-C license are deemed to be accepted upon downloading the software and/or exercising any of the rights granted under the CeCILL-C license.


## IMASViz 3.0.0 — what is new

This release turns IMASViz from a plotter into a session you can drive:

- **The agent is in the application.** One LLM-driven agent operates the gestures of the application itself — no external agent and no MCP client needed — and it is reached five ways: the *Agent* line of the application's [File menu](#the-in-app-agent), the terminal *REPL* [`viz_cli`](#the-terminal-client-viz_cli), the browser page [`viz_web`](#the-web-interface-viz_web), the [multi-user gateway](#the-multi-user-gateway-viz_gateway) (one isolated instance per user, behind a token), and as [MCP tools](#driving-imasviz-from-an-agent-mcp-tools) for external clients such as Qwen Code. The [one-shot mode](#the-one-shot-agent-mode) runs a prompt on a node without a display and leaves the pictures and the transcript on disk.
- **The multi-axis data, along the axis you want.** Any quantity can be traced where it lives — along the clock, or along the leaf's own coordinate (the `equilibrium` pressure profile as a function of `psi`), a slice at any instant (`plot_slice`), and slices stacked on the same figure with each curve's index fixed in the legend (`into_figure_key`) — the way `AGENT_HELP.md` at the repository root talks it through.
- **A major cleanup.** The defects of the earlier releases were chased down and fixed; the fixes are pinned by the test suite under `tests/` (see `TESTING.md`), which is what keeps them fixed.


## Installing and launching IMASViz from sources

### Requirements

IMASViz walks the data dictionary through the Access Layer metadata of each IDS, so it
needs the python HLI hosted at https://github.com/iterorganization/IMAS-Python.git
(the IMAS-Python module, distribution "imas-python").

IMASViz CANNOT run on the generated python HLI hosted at
ssh://git@git.iter.org/imas/al-python.git (the IMAS-AL-Python module, distribution
"imas"). Measured on IMAS-AL-Python/5.4.0: that package exports DBEntry, IDSName,
IDSBase and one module per IDS (imas.equilibrium, imas.tf, ...), and carries no
metadata reflection API at all — IDSDataType and IDSStructArray appear in none of
its files. `import imas` succeeds there, so the application says so out loud at
start-up: which package is in charge, what it lacks, and which module to load. 

IMASViz requires the following libraries:

- IMAS (the IMAS Python HLI, provided by the IMAS-Python module)

- Python3.x and the following Python libraries:
   - PySide6 (Qt for Python — the sources import PySide6, not PyQt5)
   - pyqtgraph
   - matplotlib
   - numpy. The code asks only names that NumPy 1 and NumPy 2 both answer, which is
     what lets it run on a stack built on either. That is a rule the test suite
     enforces -- a test walks every source of the package and refuses a name the
     other generation removed -- and it went unenforced once: the Equilibrium
     overview plugin asked np.unicode_, NumPy 2 has no such name, and the plugin was
     refused on a numpy 2 stack with one line in the log panel where its window
     should have been. What has *not* been verified is a whole run under numpy 2: on
     the node these sources are tested on that is impossible, because the Access
     Layer there is compiled against numpy 1 and forcing a numpy 2 makes
     ``import imas`` die in scipy with a binary incompatibility.
   - Sphinx
   (pip3 install sphinx)
   No Sphinx theme is needed: the manual is built on Sphinx's own ``basic``
   theme and dressed by ``doc/source/_static/imasviz.css``, which is in the
   repository. (It used to ask for ``sphinx_rtd_theme``, which is not installed
   on the nodes IMASViz is built on -- ``make doc`` stopped there.)


### Obtaining the source code

Clone the IMASViz repository from **https://github.com/IRFM/IMASViz.git**.

By default, the main branch will be created.

To switch to the develop branch:

    git checkout develop

### Setting the environment

To set the environment, load the modules providing IMAS, PySide6, pyqtgraph,
numpy and matplotlib, then go to the **repository root** (the top-level package
is **imasviz/**) and set **VIZ_HOME**, **VIZ_PRODUCTION**,
**IMAS_VIZ_VERSION** and **UDA_DISABLED** environment variables by executing
the following commands:

    module load ach-modules
    module load PySide6/6.6.2-GCCcore-13.2.0
    module load IMAS-Python/2.3.0-foss-2023b
    module load PyQtGraph/0.13.7-foss-2023b

    cd IMASViz

    # bash
    export VIZ_PRODUCTION=1
    export VIZ_HOME=$PWD
    export IMAS_VIZ_VERSION=2.3.7
    export UDA_DISABLED=1 #disable UDA

    # csh
    setenv VIZ_PRODUCTION 1
    setenv VIZ_HOME $PWD
    setenv UDA_DISABLED 1 #disable UDA

The block above names the **foss** HLI. On the **gateway** nodes (whose profile
pins `SciPy-bundle/2023.12-iimkl`) the foss variant fails to load that
requirement, and what runs is the **intel** HLI instead —
`IMAS-Python/2.3.0-intel-2023b`, `PyQtGraph/0.13.7-intel-2023b`. See [Starting
on the Gateway](#starting-from-sources-gateway-iter-hpc-west): the scripts in `scripts/` load that
stack and set this environment for you.

IMASViz reads the IMAS Data Dictionary at run time through the Access Layer
Python bindings (imas.ids_metadata), so no parser code is generated any more.
No dictionary file is needed either: the shot view asks the case it has opened
which data dictionary version that case is stored with (DBEntry.dd_version) and
takes the list of IDSs and their documentation from the factory of that version
(imas.IDSFactory), which the IMAS-Python distribution carries inside its own
package. A case is opened **at its own version**: an entry left unpinned settles
on the newest dictionary the package holds (4.1.1 on the stack above), and the
Access Layer then reads every IDS of a case written under another major version
while still listing its contents -- so IMASViz reads the version the URI spells
(version=3, or the <db>/<data version>/<shot>/<run> of a path case), asks the
case the exact version its first filled IDS names, and opens the entry there.
**IMAS_VERSION**, if you export it, is the version of the data
dictionary — IMAS-Python reads that variable itself — and never an Access Layer
number: on the stack above, AL_VERSION is 5.7.1 while the newest dictionary the
package holds is 4.1.1, and a version the package does not carry is refused by
name when the case is opened. It is the fallback for a case that spells no
version at all.

The repository Makefile builds the documentation only:

        # Main command (documentation)
        make

### Configuring UDA

1. The IMAS access layer used by IMASViz should support UDA for accessing remote UDA servers.

2. The file 'UDA_machines' located in directory $VIZ_HOME/config lists all tokamaks with remote UDA servers which
can be accessed from the current machine where IMASViz is installed.
Please refers to the documentation for the required prerequisites for accessing a particular remote UDA server.



### Running IMASViz from sources

The modules loaded in **Setting the environment** already provide the IMAS
Python HLI, PySide6, pyqtgraph and matplotlib, so no additional module load is
required. A graphical display (X) is needed to show the window:

    python $VIZ_HOME/imasviz/VizGUI/QtVIZ_GUI.py

### Installing and browsing IMASViz documentation

**IMASViz documentation** can be generated to multiple formats, e.g. **HTML**, **PDF**...

In $VIZ_HOME directory execute:

    make doc

    # to open PDF documentation
    xdg-open doc/build/latex/IMASViz.pdf

    # to open HTML documentation
    firefox doc/build/html/index.html




## Running IMASViz from installed modules on Gateway and ITER HPC

### GateWay HPC

Load the following modules:

    module load cineca
    module load imasenv

Check the installed IMASViz modules:

    module available imas-viz

Load a specific version of IMASViz, for example:

    module load imas-viz/2.3.7


### ITER HPC

Load the IMAS module:

    module load IMAS

Check the installed IMASViz modules:

    module available Viz

Load a specific version of IMASViz, for example:

    module load Viz/2.3.7-intel-2018a-Python-3.6.4


### Launching IMASViz

To launch **IMASViz**, execute the following command:

    viz


### IMASViz documentation

To browse IMASViz documentation, just type:

    viz_doc


### Saving and restoring a session

**File -> Save session** or **File -> Load session** (from the application window's *File* menu) allows saving and restoring what is on the screen — the cases that are open, the IDS occurrences they have loaded and the time slices each was asked for, the selection, the plot windows with the key the Windows menu and its Delete lines address them by, and the windows a plugin drew — to **$HOME/.imasviz/<name>.vsess**. 

**File -> Load session** lists those files and puts one back: the cases are reopened and the windows drawn again by the same commands that drew them the first time, so a restored window owns its sliders, its axis and its legend. What had been *set* on a window comes back with it: where its time sliders stood, which of the error lines were on and at what step, the range a plot figure was zoomed onto, and where its window sat in the area — maximized or hidden, hidden not closed. A plugin window comes back by the same rule: the file names which plugin, which entry of it, on which IDS and at which occurrence, and the restore asks that entry again with those answers, so the user is asked nothing — the window returns as its entry draws it, which means a tab added by hand is gone. See the manual, *Other GUI features -> Save/Load Session*, for what the file keeps and what it does not keep.

Closing IMASViz writes the session that was on screen to
**$HOME/.imasviz/last_session.vsess**, and the next start asks once whether to
put it back — a question that is not asked at all when no such file is there.

The IDSs of a session are read lazily through IMAS-Python, which is what makes
putting a session back affordable: on the reference case, building the tree of
one *equilibrium* occurrence costs 0,10 s and 7,7 Mo this way against 3,99 s and
122,8 Mo read whole, with the same tree either way.

## Driving IMASViz from an agent (MCP tools)

IMASViz can be driven by an MCP agent the way it is driven by its menus:
`mcp_server/` exposes the gestures of one application session as tools of the
official `mcp` SDK (v1 `FastMCP`), over `streamable_http` on a local socket.
The application stays the main program — the GUI runs its event loop and is
visible; the transport is one daemon thread, and every tool is executed on the
GUI thread through `mcp_server/dispatcher.py`, whatever thread the request
came from.

### Requirements

The same stack as the application, plus the SDK (v1 — the 2.x line renamed the
surface). On the ACH nodes whose default profile pins
`SciPy-bundle/2023.12-iimkl`, the HLI is the **intel** variant (the foss one
fails to load its SciPy-bundle requirement there; verified 2026-11-05):

    module load PySide6/6.6.2-GCCcore-13.2.0
    module load IMAS-Python/2.3.0-intel-2023b
    module load PyQtGraph/0.13.7-intel-2023b
    pip install --user "mcp[http]<2"     # FastMCP + starlette + uvicorn

### Starting the session with its tools

From the repository root, with `VIZ_HOME` and the environment as above:

    python -m mcp_server --port 8765          # the GUI comes up and is visible
    python -m mcp_server --offscreen          # a node without a display

and any MCP client pointed at the endpoint. In Qwen Code, the entry goes in
the `mcpServers` of `settings.json`, on the `httpUrl` field (the streamable
transport; `url` is the SSE one), and the server must be running before the
session starts — Qwen Code loads its MCP servers at startup:

    { "mcpServers": { "imasviz": {
          "httpUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp",
          "timeout": 600000,
          "discoveryTimeoutMs": 15000 } } }

What a tool answers is the answer of the application: a refused gesture
returns the named `ValueError` the command raises (as an error of the
call), and `get_logs(uri)` holds those refusals the way the log panel holds
them for the user.

### The tools

| Group | Tools |
| --- | --- |
| cases | `open_case`, `list_cases`, `close_case` |
| tree | `list_idss`, `list_case_idss` (the IDS the case actually holds), `load_ids`, `search_nodes`, `describe_node`, `describe_ids`, `list_quantities`, `select_signals`, `unselect_all`, `list_selection`, `select_from_configuration` |
| plotting | `plot_one` (along the clock or a leaf coordinate, `time`/`time_interp` for a slice, `into_figure_key` to stack on an open figure), `plot_slice`, `plot_selection`, `plot_table`, `plot_stacked`, `get_signal`, `save_plot_configuration` |
| windows | `list_windows`, `show_window`, `hide_window`, `close_window`, `focus_window` (bring a window to the front at a readable size) |
| plugins | `list_plugins`, `run_plugin` |
| session | `save_session`, `restore_session`, `export_to_ids`, `list_sessions`, `list_configurations`, `save_selection` |
| inspection | `get_screenshot` (PNG of a window — a figure key it does not hold is refused by name), `get_logs` (the case log panel) |
| reports | `plot_report` (one window of curves as self-contained HTML with stats), `case_report` (the whole loaded tree as HTML with stats) |

Stylage and cursors are kept in the hands of the user; they are not offered
as tools. The suite drives this layer in `tests/test_100_mcp_core_offscreen.py`
(without the SDK) and `tests/test_101_mcp_tools_http.py` (over a real
transport).

### The one-shot agent mode

The same agent on a node without a display: one prompt, the pictures it
drew and its transcript left on disk, then the process leaves. No MCP
service is served on that path, and the exit code is the verdict — 0 when
the loop answered, 1 when it refused (the refusal is named, in the
transcript and on the line that follows it):

    python -m mcp_server --agent "Ouvre le cas …, trace le champ" \
        --llm-url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1 \
        [--llm-model NAME] [--llm-key KEY] \
        [--max-turns 8] [--shots ./agent_shots] [--extra-shots app,case-views]

Each figure the run left open is saved as `<figure key>.png` (the `:` of a
key spelled `_`) beside its `transcript.txt`; `--extra-shots` names the
interface captures to save as well — `app` (`00_app.png`) and `case-views`
(`case_…`) — and a capture is an ask, never a by-product. An endpoint named
without a model takes the endpoint's first `/v1/models` entry; a figure key
the session does not hold is refused by name. `viz_cli`'s one prompt
delegates to this same path, and so does `AGENT_HELP.md`'s
walkthrough. `tests/test_112_offscreen_agent_mode.py` pins the images, the
transcript and the exit code.

### The terminal client (viz_cli)

The same gestures, from the shell, without a window: `viz_cli` is the in-app
agent on your own keyboard. It holds **one** agent session open for its whole
life — the offscreen application, its core, and one `AgentLoop` — so a prompt
can point at what the one before it named. The arrow keys, the tab-completion
and the history are the client's own (this node's Python has no C module behind
`readline`), and the history file is the one it keeps in
`$HOME/.imasviz/cli_history`, between runs, as the preferences are kept.

    python3 -m viz_cli                          # the REPL, offscreen
    python3 -m viz_cli "trace le champ"         # one prompt, then exit (0 if it
                                                # answered, 1 if it refused)

The one-shot takes the same flags as the [one-shot agent mode](#the-one-shot-agent-mode)
above — `--llm-url` / `--llm-model` / `--llm-key`, `--max-turns` (default 12,
this run's loop limit), `--shots DIR` (default `~/.imasviz/cli/shots`) and
`--history FILE` (default `~/.imasviz/cli_history`) — plus `--display` to
keep the window when you have one (offscreen is the point of the client,
the default on a node without a display).

Inside the REPL, a line runs the agent; the `/` commands steer it:

- `/help` — the commands and the gestures this instance offers;
- `/llm <field> <value>` — the model it runs against. `endpoint`, `model` and
  `key` are persisted in the **same** `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences` the GUI
  reads (`AgentLLMEndpoint`, `AgentLLMModel`, `AgentLLMApiKey`), so the two
  interfaces stay on the same endpoint; `/llm` alone reads them back.
  `max_turns`, `temperature` and `max_tokens` live on the live client, for
  that run only; `/llm model auto` makes the sent name the endpoint's first
  `/v1/models` entry, read on first use;
- `/mcp [name]` — the services this session answers today (endpoint, models,
  tools) and, for one tool, its words and its parameters
  (e.g. `/mcp plot_selection`);
- `/ids [URI]` — the IDS the open case actually holds, raw: the names and
  their occurrences read off the case's entry, with no LLM in between
  (with no URI, the one open case, refused by name when there is no
  single one);
- `/session` — the cases open in this session, or `/session save <name>` and
  `/session restore <name>` to write and put one back;
- `/shots [dir]` — where the turns' images and transcripts are saved;
- `/history` — the prompts of this client run;
- `/quit` — leave (the arrows keep your prompts for the next run).

What a turn answers is the answer of the application the way `test_100` pins
it: a refused gesture is named, and `get_logs` holds those refusals; the
REPL itself is pinned by `tests/test_113_viz_cli.py`, and the one prompt
behaves the way `tests/test_112_offscreen_agent_mode.py` pins the
[one-shot agent mode](#the-one-shot-agent-mode).

### The web interface (viz_web)

The same gestures, from a browser — and the IMASViz window stays offscreen:
a node without a display (a tablet) drives the whole session through one
self-contained page, `viz_web/static/index.html`, no build step and no new
dependency (it is served by the `starlette` + `uvicorn` the MCP server
already required).

    python3 -m viz_web                          # on http://127.0.0.1:8770
    python3 -m viz_web --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8771   # another device on the box

`--offscreen` forces the offscreen platform even when a `DISPLAY` is set (it
is the default when there is none); `--llm-url` / `--llm-model` /
`--llm-key` name the local model for the run, otherwise the `AgentLLM*`
lines of `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences` stand, the same file the REPL and the
in-app agent read; `--max-turns` (default 12) is that run's loop limit, and
`--shots DIR` names where the turns' images and transcripts go (default
`~/.imasviz/web/shots`). A device on the network points its browser at the
box's address and port.

The page is the REPL with a picture. The left column runs the agent: a
prompt, the transcript streamed in the order the loop writes it — refusals
included, in the log colour they wear in the panel — the *Requests* list
naming the turns the session has had, and beneath it a closed *Raw
gestures* group (the rarely used one), one form per tool with its
parameters, run the way the loop runs them (so `plot_one` given an
argument it does not take is refused by name, the names it does hold
beside it). The right column re-draws under *Screenshots* and *Reports*
what each turn left on disk in `~/.imasviz/web/shots/<n>/` — its
transcript, and the PNG of the application and of each of its windows:
when the agent draws or names a figure it calls `focus_window` on it, and
a focus is a capture ask, so the figure lands in the page's gallery and
the previous turns' pictures stay, folded under their name. The model's
running parameters are read from and written to the same preferences
through the page's own `/llm` line.

The suite drives it in `tests/test_119_viz_web_session.py` (one turn at a
time, the refusals named, the context between turns),
`tests/test_120_viz_web_routes.py` (the routes, the stream, the shots read
back), `tests/test_121_viz_web_house_style.py` (the page wears the
application's colours in both themes, and nothing of it imports a web
framework this node does not hold),
`tests/test_127_web_show_focus_and_report.py` (show, focus and report),
`tests/test_128_web_redraw_captured.py` (a turn's stack on an open figure
re-draws it), `tests/test_129_web_turn_keeps_the_session_on_screen.py` and
`tests/test_131_web_figures_visible_and_kept.py` (the figures the agent
names are shown and kept, the way `tests/test_136_agent_shows_the_figures_it_names.py`
pins the loop itself).

### The multi-user gateway (viz_gateway)

One browser per user at the same time. `viz_gateway` wraps `viz_web` so a
node answers **more than one user at a time** — and it does not modify
them: `imasviz/`, `viz_web/`, `viz_cli/` and `mcp_server/` do not budge.
The model is one instance per user: a *local* user's token answers on a
`viz_web` the gateway spawns, isolated by its own process and its own
`$HOME` (under `~/.imasviz/gateway/homes/<user>`); a *remote* user's token
points at a backend URL that has its own process and configuration
elsewhere. The gateway itself owns **no** widget — it is an HTTP proxy and
a process supervisor, and the children it spawns inherit the module stack
the launching shell loaded, changing only their `HOME`.

Start it from a shell that already loaded the HLI stack (the *Gateway*
block of [Setting the environment](#setting-the-environment) above, or the
site's launcher minus the launch itself), then:

    python3 -m viz_gateway --port 8760 --max-users 20 --max-turns 12 --idle-timeout 1800

The users sit in `~/.imasviz/gateway/users.json` (token → user → backend),
written by hand or scripted through `viz_gateway.userstore.UserStore`. A
browser is pointed at `http://127.0.0.1:8760`: the login page takes the
token in, keeps it in an `HttpOnly` cookie (`imasviz_token`), and then the
page, the agent stream and the tool calls are all proxied through. For a
script: the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header, or `POST /login` with
`{"token": "…"}` to collect the cookie.

The budget counts live instances, not ports: `--max-users` (default 20)
caps how many local instances may be alive at once (`--max-users` is also
the answer to "how many users on this node?"), and an instance idle for
`--idle-timeout` (default 1800 s) is reaped — the token survives, the
session (cases, figures, selection) does not, and the user's next request
restarts one behind its token without the caller noticing. The real limit
is memory, one Qt process per user (≈ 150–300 Mo), long before the port
space runs out. `--max-turns` (default 12) is forwarded to every local
instance it spawns, and stays changeable on a live one with
`POST /api/llm`.

The refusals are named the way the application names them: a `401` naming
both the token it was given and the users the file holds, a `503` naming
the live instances and the budget, and a backend that does not come up
naming its port, its `HOME` and its exit code. The known limit of v1:
local backends listen on `127.0.0.1`, a socket any account on the box can
technically reach — the token controls the *content*, and the route to a
per-user socket is the v2 plan, spelled in `viz_gateway/README.md` with the
doctrine. The how-to is `viz_gateway/USAGE.md`, and what to say to the
agent about the multi-axis plots is `AGENT_HELP.md` at the repository
root. The suite pins it in `tests/test_127_gateway_token.py` (the catalog,
the refusals by name), `tests/test_128_gateway_proxy_streams.py` (the
proxy against a real stub backend, the SSE stream end to end),
`tests/test_129_gateway_pool.py` (one instance and one port per user, the
budget, the reaping) and
`tests/test_130_gateway_two_real_users_are_isolated.py`
(`@pytest.mark.case` — two **real** `viz_web` instances the spawner
launches: the case user A opens, user B does not see).

## Starting from sources (Gateway, ITER HPC, WEST)

Each site has its own launcher pair in `scripts/` — one for the **visible
application** (needs X) and one for the **terminal client** (offscreen, or
with `--display`). All use `VIZ_PRODUCTION=1` and locate `VIZ_HOME` from the
script's own path, so they can be run from anywhere. `module` is a shell
function and is never piped — a pipe would run the load in a subshell and lose
the environment it sets.

### Gateway (EUFUS)

Loads the **intel** HLI (the foss variant does not load its SciPy-bundle
requirement here).

    scripts/run_imasviz.sh                      # the application, with its window
    scripts/run_viz_cli.sh                      # the terminal client
    scripts/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"         # one-shot, exit 0/1

The web page and the multi-user gateway serve on exactly this stack — load
the same modules (the launchers above already do, minus the launch), then
`python3 -m viz_web --port 8770` (or `python3 -m viz_web --host 0.0.0.0`
for a browser on another device) and/or
`python3 -m viz_gateway --port 8760 --max-users 20`: see
[The web interface](#the-web-interface-viz_web) and
[The multi-user gateway](#the-multi-user-gateway-viz_gateway) above. The
one-shot prompt of the [in-app agent](#the-one-shot-agent-mode) works on
this node without a display:
`python -m mcp_server --agent "Ouvre le cas …, trace …" --shots ./shot`.

### ITER HPC

Loads the four standard modules (no version pin, the profile resolves the
active stack).

    scripts/iter/run_imasviz.sh                 # the application, with its window
    scripts/iter/run_viz_cli.sh                 # the terminal client
    scripts/iter/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"    # one-shot

### WEST

Creates a Python virtualenv at `~/.imasviz/venv` on first run, pip-installs
`IMAS-Python`, `PySide6`, `PyQtGraph` and `matplotlib` into it (the stack is
re-verified on every run), then loads the IMAS core with `module load IMAS`.

    scripts/west/run_imasviz.sh                 # the application, with its window
    scripts/west/run_viz_cli.sh                 # the terminal client
    scripts/west/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"    # one-shot

All six launchers set, for the production run: `VIZ_HOME`, `VIZ_PRODUCTION=1`,
`UDA_DISABLED=1`, `TS_MAPPINGS_DIR` (default `$repo/ts_mapping_files`) and
`IMAS_VIZ_VERSION` (overridable from the environment). They do **not** replace
the modules' `PYTHONPATH`; the code appends `VIZ_HOME` to `sys.path` itself.
`run_viz_cli.sh` hands any argument straight to `viz_cli`, so it is the one to
start a one-shot prompt from the shell.

The web interface keeps no site launcher of its own: it starts with
`python3 -m viz_web` once the same loading above holds, on the one condition
that `starlette` and `uvicorn` import (on Gateway, the MCP requirement's
`pip install --user "mcp[http]<2"` already brought them; elsewhere they come
from the same line, or from the site's virtualenv).

## The in-app agent

IMASViz can run the agent itself: the application talks to a local
OpenAI-compatible server — `llama-server`, Ollama, or any endpoint that
answers `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/models` — and drives its own
gestures through the same `McpGuiCore` the MCP surface uses. No external
agent or MCP client is needed in production.

It is opened from the **Agent** line of the application's *File* menu:
one window per session — the entry reuses it on every click after the
first rather than rebuilding a second loop on the same api — and it sits
in the *Windows* menu as `Agent:1`, alongside the figures the user drew.
Type a prompt, click *Tourner*, and the LLM chooses the gestures — the
transcript shows each step, and refusals are named the same way they
appear in the log panel; *Arrêter* stops the turn at the next tool
boundary and *Effacer* clears the transcript.

Configuration (in `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences`, the values the code ships):

    AgentLLMEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1   # point it at any OpenAI-compatible server
    AgentLLMModel=qwen3.8:27b                     # empty = the endpoint's first /v1/models entry
    AgentLLMApiKey=                               # empty = no auth header

The suite drives the agent loop and panel in `tests/test_110_agent_loop.py`
(scripted LLM, no network) and `tests/test_111_agent_panel.py` (widget,
registered in `figureframes`).
